---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="OZ 1.0 Retrospective" description="What the evidence says about OZ 1.0 outcomes — investment patterns, community impact, and lessons for OZ 2.0.">
  <div class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4">OZ 1.0 Retrospective</h1>
    <p class="text-oz-neutral-500 mb-8">
      The original Opportunity Zone program ran from 2018 under the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act.
      A body of academic research, government assessments, and community development critiques
      now exists on what it did and didn't accomplish. OZ 2.0 was designed in explicit response
      to those findings. This page summarizes the evidence honestly.
    </p>

    <section id="investment-data" class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">What the investment data showed<a href="#investment-data" class="anchor-link" aria-label="Copy link to section"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"/><path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"/></svg></a></h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-oz-neutral-700 mb-4">
        Significant private capital did flow into OZ-designated tracts. Qualified Opportunity Funds
        (QOFs) self-certified with the IRS via Form 8996, and Treasury's filings showed meaningful
        fund formation and asset accumulation. The Trump White House Council of Economic Advisers,
        in its
        <a href="https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/The-Impact-of-Opportunity-Zones-An-Initial-Assessment.pdf" class="text-oz-blue underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">August 2020 initial assessment</a>,
        characterized the program as generating substantial investment and job creation in
        designated areas, drawing on early Form 8996 returns and survey data.
        Treasury economists Coyne and Johnson, with access to the full Form 8996 universe,
        documented approximately $48 billion in total QOF assets by end of 2020 — rising
        from $4 billion in 2018 to $26 billion in 2019 before moderating to $18 billion in
        new annual flow in 2020.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-oz-neutral-700 mb-4">
        The geographic distribution of that capital, however, was skewed. Kennedy and Wheeler,
        using Form 8996 data accessed via the Joint Committee on Taxation, found that
        <strong class="text-oz-neutral-900">84% of designated OZ tracts received zero
        investment</strong> in 2019, with the top 1% of tracts absorbing 42% of all capital
        and the top 5% taking roughly 80%. Investment concentrated in tracts that were already
        showing signs of appreciation — often urban OZs in or near gentrifying neighborhoods,
        not the high-distress rural and legacy industrial tracts the program was originally
        conceived to help. By 2020, only
        <strong class="text-oz-neutral-900">48% of designated communities had attracted any
        capital at all</strong> (Coyne &amp; Johnson). Rural and tribal OZs attracted little
        capital relative to their share of designated tracts. EIG's own
        <a href="https://eig.org/opportunity-zones-research-brief/" class="text-oz-blue underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">January 2024 literature review</a>
        acknowledged this geographic divergence and argued for program reforms to address it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-oz-neutral-700">
        The absence of enforceable disclosure requirements under OZ 1.0 made it difficult to
        assess aggregate outcomes with precision. QOFs filed Form 8996 but faced no meaningful
        penalty for non-filing or incomplete data, and no tract-level investment reporting was
        required. The data gaps are a recurring limitation in every study of OZ 1.0 outcomes.
        Coyne and Johnson estimated roughly 25,000 individual OZ investors in 2020, with a
        median adjusted gross income of approximately $730,000 — consistent with the program's
        primary beneficiaries being high-income households with realized capital gains, not
        community-based development organizations.
      </p>
    </section>

